                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/Langlands+correspondence+--+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %p
            The term
            %em S-duality
            can mean two different things:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
                it is short for
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spanier-Whitehead+duality"} Spanier-Whitehead duality
                \:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} monoidal duality
                in the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} stable homotopy category
            %li
              %p
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
                it denotes a certain equivalence between
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
          %hr/
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_super_yangmills_theory"} In (super) Yang-Mills theory
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#general_idea"} General idea
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#ForSYMFromCompactification"} From compactification of the 6d (2,0)-SCFT and AGT correspondence
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_string_theory"} In string theory
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#InTypeIIB"} Type IIB S-duality
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a{:href => "#general_idea_2"} General idea
                    %li
                      %a{:href => "#CohomologicalNatureOfTypeIIFieldsUnderSDuality"} Cohomological nature of type II fields under S-duality
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#heterotictype_i_duality"} Heterotic/type I duality
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#for_type_iia"} For type IIA
              %li
                %a{:href => "#overview"} Overview
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesInSYM"} In (super-)Yang-Mills theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_type_ii_superstring_theory"} In type II superstring theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_heterotic_string_theory"} In heterotic string theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_geometric_langlands_duality"} Relation to geometric Langlands duality
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the original and restricted sense,
            %em S-duality
            refers to the conjectured
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Montonen-Olive+duality"} Montonen-Olive duality
            auto-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"}> super
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            in 4 dimensions under which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            is inverted, and more generally under which the combined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theta+angle"} theta angle
            tranform under an action of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+group"} modular group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            this conjecture can be argued for in detail.
          %p
            There is also a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
            called
            = succeed "." do
              %em S-duality
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+superstring+theory"} type IIB superstring theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            this is given by an action of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+group"} modular group
            on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axio-dilaton"} axio-dilaton
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+coupling+constant"} string coupling constant
          %p
            Indeed, at least for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            Montonen-Olive S-duality may be understood as a special case of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} string duality
            (
            %a{:href => "#Witten95a"}> Witten 95a
            ,
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Witten95b"} Witten 95b
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D2+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=2 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            torus (
            %a{:href => "#Johnson97"}> Johnson 97
            ) to get the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-brane"} D3-brane
            worldvolume theory, and the remnant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+group"} modular group
            action on the compactified torus is supposed to be the 4d Montonen-Olive S-duality (
            %a{:href => "#Witten07"}> Witten 07
            ).
          %h3#in_super_yangmills_theory In (super) Yang-Mills theory
          %h4#general_idea General idea
          %p
            In its original form, S-duality refers to
            %strong Montonen-Olive duality
            , which is about the following phenomenon:
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            has two summands,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi YM
                  %mo :
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mi i
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{YM} : \nabla \mapsto \int_X \frac{1}{e^2} \langle F_\nabla \wedge \star F_\nabla\rangle
                  + \int_{X} i \theta \langle F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla \rangle
                  \\,,
          %p
            each pairing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form with itself in an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
            dual, and the second not. One can combine the coefficients
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{e^2}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \theta
            into a single
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mi θ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tau = \frac{\theta}{2 \pi} +  \frac{4 \pi i}{e^2}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Montonen-Olive duality asserts that the quantum field theories induced from one such parameter value and another one obtained from it by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SL%282%2CZ%29"} SL(2,Z)
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/upper+half+plane"} upper half plane
            are equivalent.
          %p
            This is actually not quite true for ordinary Yang-Mills theory, but seems to be true for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D2+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=2 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
          %h4#ForSYMFromCompactification From compactification of the 6d (2,0)-SCFT and AGT correspondence
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Witten95a"}> Witten 95a
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten95b"} Witten 95b
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Witten07"} Witten 07
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D2+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=2 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
            may be understood geometrically by regarding the super Yang-Mills theory as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            – that instead of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            involves a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+2-bundle"} 2-connection
            – on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+torus"} complex torus
            = succeed "-invariance" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL(2,\mathbb{Z})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+group"} modular group
            remnant of the conformal invariance of the 6-dimensional theory under conformal transformations of that torus.
          %p
            Moreover, Witten has suggested that this S-duality secretly drives a host of other subtle phenomena, notably that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+duality"} geometric Langlands duality
            (see there for more) is just an aspect of a special case of this.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AGT+correspondence"} AGT correspondence
            refines this further and regards the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            as something like a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            with values in 4d super-Yang-Mills theories”. This way the whole
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+class+group"} mapping class group
            of general 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
            acts as a generalized S-duality on 4d super-Yang-Mills theory
          %h3#in_string_theory In string theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            be S-dual to itself and make
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            be S-dual to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I+string+theory"} type I string theory
          %h4#InTypeIIB Type IIB S-duality
          %h5#general_idea_2 General idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Type+IIB+string+theory"} Type IIB string theory
            is obtained by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            bundle followed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-dualizing
            one of the torus cycles. This perspective – referred to as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            – exhibits the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axio-dilaton"} axio-dilaton
            of type IIB string theory as the fiber of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+fibration"} elliptic fibration
            (essentially the torus bundle that M-theory was compactified on (
            %a{:href => "#Johnson97"}> Johnson 97
            )).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+group"} modular group
            acts on this
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+fibration"} elliptic fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau \mapsto - \frac{1}{\tau}
            inverts the type II coupling constant. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            for more.
          %p
            The type IIB
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F1-string"} F1-string
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D1-brane"} D1-brane
            appear this way by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            wrapping (either) one of the two cycles of the compactifying torus. S-duality mixes these strings by the evident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+group"} modular group
            action on the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)\in \mathbb{Z}^2
            labels of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28p%2Cq%29-strings"} (p,q)-strings
            = succeed "-strings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-infinity+algebras"} super L-infinity algebras
            which defines the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+super+spacetime"} extended super spacetime
            constituted by the type II superstring (
            %a{:href => "Bandos00"}> Bandos 00
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FSS13"} FSS 13, section 4.3
          %p
            Similarly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D5-brane"} D5-brane
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-brane"} NS5-brane
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            wrapping one of the two cycles of the compactifying torus, and hence the S-duality modular group also acts on
            = succeed "-5-branes," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
          %p
            Finally, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-brane"} D3-brane
            is instead the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            %em both
            compactifying dimensions. Accordingly the worldvolume theory of the D3, which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 4
            has an S-self-duality. That is supposed to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Montonen-Olive+duality"} Montonen-Olive duality
            discussed above, which is thereby unified with type IIB S-duality.
          %h5#CohomologicalNatureOfTypeIIFieldsUnderSDuality Cohomological nature of type II fields under S-duality
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            does capture much of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative
            S-duality, there currently remains a puzzle as to the correct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            nature of all the fields under S-duality: by the above S-duality mixes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi NS
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat B_{NS}
            with the degree-3 component
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi RR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat B_{RR}
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi NS
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat B_{NS}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"}> twisted
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi RR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat B_{RR}
            is (only) one component of a cocycle in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"}> twisted
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            (or really:
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KR-theory"} KR-theory
          %p
            This issue was first highlighted in (
            %a{:href => "#DMW00"}> DMW 00, section 11
            ). In (
            %a{:href => "supergravity+C-field#DFM"}> DFM 03, section 9
            ) it was observed that taking into account the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+structure+in+M-theory"} cubical structure in M-theory
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} 11-dimensional Chern-Simons term
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            the conceptual mismatch is alleviated, but not quite resolved. See also (
            %a{:href => "#BEJVS05"}> BEJVS 05
            )
          %p
            On the other hand, as discussed at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+structure+in+M-theory"} cubical structure in M-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
            beyond
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            and beyond
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#KrizSati05"} KrizSati 05
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"} conjecture
            that the right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            to capture the S-duality of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring+theory"} type IIB
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+equivariant+elliptic+cohomology"} modular equivariant elliptic cohomology
          %h4#heterotictype_i_duality Heterotic/type I duality
          %blockquote
            %p
              Something substantial should go here, for the moment the following is copied from a discussion forum comment by some Olof
              = succeed ":" do
                %a{:href => "http://physics.stackexchange.com/a/65546/5603"} here
          %p For the Het/I relation, the first observation is that the massless spectra of the two models agree. Moreover, if we make the identification
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mi ν
                    %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mi Φ
                        %mi h
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mi ν
                    %mi h
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi h
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mover
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 3
                    %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 3
                    %mi h
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                    %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                    %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G^I_{\mu\nu} = e^{-\Phi_h} G^h_{\mu\nu} , \qquad
                  \\Phi^I = - \Phi^h , \qquad
                  \\tilde{F}^I_3 = \tilde{H}^h_3 , \qquad
                  A^I_1 = A^h_1
          %p
            the low energy effective supergravity actions of the two models match. Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+coupling+constants"} string coupling constants
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mi s
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_s^I
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mi s
                    %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_s^h
            are given as the expectation values of the exponentials of the dilatons
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\Phi^I)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Φ
                      %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\Phi^h)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mi s
                    %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mi s
                        %mi h
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g^I_s = \frac{1}{g^h_s} .
          %p From the relative scaling of the metric in (1) we also see that the string length in the two theories are related by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi l
                    %mi s
                    %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi l
                    %mi s
                    %mi h
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mi s
                        %mi h
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  l^I_s = l^h_s \sqrt{g^h_s}.
          %p
            As a non-perturbative check we can consider the tension of the type-I D1 brane. The brane is a BPS object, so for all values of the coupling
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mi s
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_s^I
            the tension is given by the same formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                    %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mi s
                        %mi I
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi l
                            %mi s
                            %mi I
                          %mo )
                        %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mi s
                        %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi l
                            %mi s
                            %mi h
                          %msqrt
                            %mrow
                              %msubsup
                                %mi g
                                %mi s
                                %mi h
                          %mo )
                        %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi l
                            %mi s
                            %mi h
                          %mo )
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T^I_{D1} = \frac{1}{g_s^I} \frac{1}{2\pi\left(l^I_s\right)^2} = \frac{g^h_s}{2\pi\left(l^h_s\sqrt{g^h_s}\right)^2} = \frac{1}{2\pi\left(l^h_s\right)^2}
          %p where I’ve used relations (2) and (3). But this is equal to the tension of the fundamental heterotic string
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mn 1
                    %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi l
                            %mi s
                            %mi h
                          %mo )
                        %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T^h_{F1} = \frac{1}{2\pi\left(l^h_s\right)^2}.
          %p This indicates that it is sensible to identify the strong coupling limit of the type-I D1 brane with the heterotic string.
          %h4#for_type_iia For type IIA
          %p
            A priori
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+superstring+theory"} type IIA superstring theory
            does not have S-duality, but by compactifying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            on a torus one can sort of read off what the non-perturbative additions to type IIA should be that make it have S-duality after all, see
          %ul
            %li
              Gottfried Curio, Boris Kors,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dieter+L%C3%BCst"} Dieter Lüst
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fluxes and Branes in Type II Vacua and M-theory Geometry with G(2) and Spin(7) Holonomy
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0111165"} arXiv:hep-th/0111165
